# MDEV-38814: exercise the cross-product of the two independent gates that
# reduce index tree lock SX-to-X upgrades, innodb_index_shrink and
# innodb_index_lock_upgrade_root. Each combination sets both variables; the
# first ([on]) is the all-legacy baseline recorded as the base .result, the
# rest are recorded as rdiffs against it.
#
# innodb_index_shrink=OFF: all DB_UNDERFLOW fallbacks on growing records are
# eliminated, DB_OVERFLOW fallbacks on growing records are forced to split when
# the page cannot satisfy BTR_CUR_PAGE_REORGANIZE_LIMIT, and the index-lock
# upgrade count drops accordingly on the same workload. Same-size record
# UPDATEs are unaffected. Shrinking-record UPDATEs are marginally affected,
# because they occur after the record-growing UPDATEs and an extra page is
# produced via split as a side-effect, so they operate on a slightly different
# B-tree.
#
# innodb_index_lock_upgrade_root=OFF: btr_cur_need_opposite_intention() returns
# false for the root page (which has no parent or siblings, so a structural
# change cannot cascade upward), removing the SX-to-X upgrades that the root
# would otherwise force. need_opposite_intention_root counts the root
# encounters either way; n_index_lock_upgrades is what drops.
#
# Compressed pages (t3) get the innodb_index_shrink optimistic-side change but
# not the pessimistic-side one, by design. The optimistic gate
# (page_get_data_size vs BTR_CUR_PAGE_COMPRESS_LIMIT) is an uncompressed-frame
# metric that translates cleanly to compressed pages, so it applies uniformly.
# The pessimistic gate (page_get_max_insert_size_after_reorganize vs
# BTR_CUR_PAGE_REORGANIZE_LIMIT) is also an uncompressed-frame metric, but the
# actual constraint on a compressed page is whether the compressed image fits
# in KEY_BLOCK_SIZE after recompress, a different question. Using the
# uncompressed metric there would mispredict in both directions, so
# btr_cur_pessimistic_update() falls through to the legacy
# btr_cur_insert_if_possible() path, which already handles compressed-page
# complexity. The innodb_index_lock_upgrade_root gate is page-position based
# (root vs non-root) and therefore applies uniformly to t3.

[on]
--loose-innodb-index-shrink=ON
--loose-innodb-index-lock-upgrade-root=ON

[shrink_off]
--loose-innodb-index-shrink=OFF
--loose-innodb-index-lock-upgrade-root=ON

[root_off]
--loose-innodb-index-shrink=ON
--loose-innodb-index-lock-upgrade-root=OFF

[both_off]
--loose-innodb-index-shrink=OFF
--loose-innodb-index-lock-upgrade-root=OFF
